First Author: Fessler
Title: Machine Learning for Predicting Pulmonary Graft Dysfunction After Double-Lung Transplantation: A Single-Center Study Using Donor, Recipient, and Intraoperative Variables
Source number: 63

Very short summary: This retrospective single-center cohort study analysed prospectively collected perioperative data from 477 double-lung transplant recipients in France between 2012 and 2019. XGBoost models were developed at nine sequential perioperative time points to predict grade 3 primary graft dysfunction at 72 hours; the optimal six-feature model at second graft implantation achieved an AUROC of 0.84, sensitivity of 0.81, and specificity of 0.68, although feature selection was not nested and no external validation was performed.

